window.BAHR_DATA=window.BAHR_DATA||{};window.BAHR_DATA["passages-07"]=[{"id":"q2-109-110","q":"2:109–110","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:109–110 · Pardon, worship and freedom from preoccupation with opponents","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 151–152","cairo":"I:151–152","source_page_first":151,"source_page_last":152,"ar":"وَدَّ كَثِيرٌ مِنْ أَهْلِ الْكِتابِ لَوْ يَرُدُّونَكُمْ مِنْ بَعْدِ إِيمانِكُمْ كُفَّاراً حَسَداً مِنْ عِنْدِ أَنْفُسِهِمْ مِنْ بَعْدِ ما تَبَيَّنَ لَهُمُ الْحَقُّ فَاعْفُوا وَاصْفَحُوا حَتَّى يَأْتِيَ اللَّهُ بِأَمْرِهِ إِنَّ اللَّهَ عَلى كُلِّ شَيْءٍ قَدِيرٌ (109) وَأَقِيمُوا الصَّلاةَ وَآتُوا الزَّكاةَ وَما تُقَدِّمُوا لِأَنْفُسِكُمْ مِنْ خَيْرٍ تَجِدُوهُ عِنْدَ اللَّهِ إِنَّ اللَّهَ بِما تَعْمَلُونَ بَصِيرٌ (110)\nقلت: لَوْ مصدريةٌ مفعول «وَدَّ» ، وكُفَّاراً: مفعول ثان، وحَسَداً: مفعول له، علة لود، أو حال من الواو، ومِنْ عِنْدِ متعلق بود، أي: يتمنوا ذلك من عند أنفسهم وتَشهِّيهم، أو بقوله: «حسداً» ، فالوقف على قوله كُفَّاراً، أي: حسداً حاصلاً من تلقاء أنفسهم، لم يستندوا فيه إلى شبهة ولا دليل، والعفو: ترك العقوبة بالذنب. والصفح: الإعراض عن المذنب، كأنه يولي عنه صفحة عنقه، فهو أبلغ من العفو.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله في التحذير من اليهود وغيرهم من الكفار: تمنّى الذين كفروا من أهل الكتاب وغيرهم لو يصرفونكم عن دينكم ويَرُدُّونَكُمْ مِنْ بَعْدِ إِيمانِكُمْ بنبيكم كُفَّاراً ضالين، كما كنتم قبل الدخول فيه، وذلك حَسَداً مِنْ تلقاء أَنْفُسِهِمْ غيرة أن تكون النبوة في غيرهم، وذلك مِنْ بَعْدِ ما تَبَيَّنَ لَهُمُ الْحَقُّ وعرفوه كما يعرفون أبناءهم، فَاعْفُوا عن عتابهم، وأعرضوا عن تشغيبهم حَتَّى يَأْتِيَ اللَّهُ بِأَمْرِهِ فيهم بالقتل والجلاء. إِنَّ اللَّهَ عَلى كُلِّ شَيْءٍ قَدِيرٌ، واشتغلوا بما كلفكم به من أداء حقوق العبودية، والقيام بوظائف الربوبية، كإتقان الصلاة وأداء الزكاة، واعلموا أن الله لا يضيع من أعمالكم شيئاً، فما تقدموا لأنفسكم ليوم فقركم تجدوه عند الله خيرا وأعظم أجراً، إن الله لا يخفى عليه شيء من أعمالكم وأحوالكم.\nنزلت الآية في عمار بن ياسر وحذيفة بن اليمان، أتيا بيت المِدْرَاس ، فألانوا لهم الكلام، فطمعوا في صرفهما عن دينهما، ففضحهم الله ورد كيدهم في نحرهم. والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: من جملة ما دَبَّ إلى بعض الطوائف المتجمدين على تقليد أشياخهم: التعصب والحمية على طريق أشياخهم، ولو ظهر الحق عند غيرهم، وخصوصاً أولاد الصالحين منهم، فإذا رأوا أحداً ظهرت عليه أنوار الولاية،\nوأسرار الخصوصية، تمنَّوْا أن يردوهم عن طريق الحق، ويصرفوهم إلى مخالطة الخلق، حسداً من عند أنفسهم من بعد ما تبيّن لهم الحق، فيقال لمن توجه إلى الحق: فاعفوا واصفحوا حتى يظهر الحق، ولا تلتفتوا إلى تشغيبهم، ولا تشتغلوا قط بعيبهم فتكونوا أقبح منهم.\nقال بعض العارفين: (لا تشتغل قط بمَن يؤذيك واشتغل بالله يرده عنك، وقد غلط في هذا الأمر خلق كثير، اشتغلوا بمن يؤذيهم فطال الأذى مع الإثم. ولو أنهم رجعوا إلى مولاهم لكفاهم أمرهم.) . بل ينبغي لمن يُحْسَدُ أو يُؤْذَى أن يغيب عن الحاسد وكيده، ويشتغل بما هو مكلف به من حقوق العبودية وشهود عظمة الربوبية، فإن الله لا يضيع مَن التجأ إليه، ولا يخيب مقصود من اعتمد عليه. وبالله التوفيق.\nومن جملة أمانى اليهود الفارغة: ادعاء اختصاصهم بالجنة، كما أبان ذلك الحق تعالى بقوله:","quran_ar":"وَدَّ كَثِيرٌ مِنْ أَهْلِ الْكِتابِ لَوْ يَرُدُّونَكُمْ مِنْ بَعْدِ إِيمانِكُمْ كُفَّاراً حَسَداً مِنْ عِنْدِ أَنْفُسِهِمْ مِنْ بَعْدِ ما تَبَيَّنَ لَهُمُ الْحَقُّ فَاعْفُوا وَاصْفَحُوا حَتَّى يَأْتِيَ اللَّهُ بِأَمْرِهِ إِنَّ اللَّهَ عَلى كُلِّ شَيْءٍ قَدِيرٌ (109) وَأَقِيمُوا الصَّلاةَ وَآتُوا الزَّكاةَ وَما تُقَدِّمُوا لِأَنْفُسِكُمْ مِنْ خَيْرٍ تَجِدُوهُ عِنْدَ اللَّهِ إِنَّ اللَّهَ بِما تَعْمَلُونَ بَصِيرٌ (110)","quran_en":"109. Many of the People of the Book wish to turn you back into unbelievers after your faith, out of envy arising from themselves, after the truth has become clear to them. Pardon and overlook until God brings His command. God has power over everything. 110. Establish prayer and give the purifying alms. Whatever good you send ahead for yourselves, you will find it with God. God sees what you do.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Pardoning and overlooking","html":"<p>I say: <i>law<\/i> is infinitival, forming the object of “wish.” “Unbelievers” is the second object of “turn back.” “Out of envy” is an accusative of purpose explaining “wish,” or circumstantial with respect to its plural subject. “From themselves” attaches either to “wish”—they wish it from themselves and their own inclination—or to “envy,” in which case one pauses after “unbelievers”: an envy arising from within, supported by neither a semblance of proof nor a demonstration. Pardoning (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العفو<\/bdi>) is refraining from punishing an offence; overlooking (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الصفح<\/bdi>) is turning away from the offender, as though showing him the side of one’s neck. It is therefore more emphatic than pardoning.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: law is infinitival, forming the object of “wish.” “Unbelievers” is the second object of “turn back.” “Out of envy” is an accusative of purpose explaining “wish,” or circumstantial with respect to its plural subject. “From themselves” attaches either to “wish”—they wish it from themselves and their own inclination—or to “envy,” in which case one pauses after “unbelievers”: an envy arising from within, supported by neither a semblance of proof nor a demonstration. Pardoning ( العفو ) is refraining from punishing an offence; overlooking ( الصفح ) is turning away from the offender, as though showing him the side of one’s neck. It is therefore more emphatic than pardoning."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Fulfil the duties entrusted to you","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, warns against the Jews and other unbelievers: the unbelieving People of the Book and others wish to turn you from your religion and make you unbelievers again after faith in your Prophet, astray as before entering it. This arises from their own envy that prophethood should be among others, after the truth has become clear and they know it as they know their sons. Refrain from reproaching them and turn away from their contentiousness until God brings His command concerning them, through killing and expulsion.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-75\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B071]<\/a> God has power over everything.<\/p><p>Occupy yourselves with what He has entrusted to you: rendering the rights of servanthood and performing the duties owed to Lordship, such as perfecting prayer and paying the alms. Know that God loses none of your works. Whatever you send ahead for your day of need, you will find with Him, better and greater in reward. None of your deeds or states is hidden from Him.<\/p><p>The verse was revealed concerning ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir and Ḥudhayfa ibn al-Yamān, who visited the house of study (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">بيت المدراس<\/bdi>). Its people spoke gently to them and hoped to divert them from their religion. God exposed them and turned their plot back upon them. And God Most High knows best.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, warns against the Jews and other unbelievers: the unbelieving People of the Book and others wish to turn you from your religion and make you unbelievers again after faith in your Prophet, astray as before entering it. This arises from their own envy that prophethood should be among others, after the truth has become clear and they know it as they know their sons. Refrain from reproaching them and turn away from their contentiousness until God brings His command concerning them, through killing and expulsion. [B071] God has power over everything. Occupy yourselves with what He has entrusted to you: rendering the rights of servanthood and performing the duties owed to Lordship, such as perfecting prayer and paying the alms. Know that God loses none of your works. Whatever you send ahead for your day of need, you will find with Him, better and greater in reward. None of your deeds or states is hidden from Him. The verse was revealed concerning ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir and Ḥudhayfa ibn al-Yamān, who visited the house of study ( بيت المدراس ). Its people spoke gently to them and hoped to divert them from their religion. God exposed them and turned their plot back upon them. And God Most High knows best."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Partisanship towards one’s teachers","html":"<p>Among the things that have crept into groups rigidly attached to imitating their shaykhs are partisanship and zeal for their shaykhs’ way, even when truth appears with others—especially among descendants of the righteous. When they see someone upon whom the lights of friendship with God<\/p><p>and the secrets of special election have appeared, they wish to turn him from the way of truth back to mingling with people, through envy from within, after truth has become clear. Those turning towards the Real are told: pardon and overlook until truth appears. Do not attend to their contentiousness or occupy yourselves with their faults, lest you become worse than they.<\/p><p>A gnostic said: “Never occupy yourself with someone who harms you; occupy yourself with God and He will turn that person from you. Many have erred here: they occupied themselves with those who harmed them, and the harm lasted, accompanied by sin. Had they returned to their Master, He would have sufficed them.” Someone envied or harmed should instead become absent from the envier and his plot and attend to the rights of servanthood and witnessing the grandeur of Lordship. God does not abandon one who takes refuge with Him, nor frustrate the aim of one who relies upon Him. Through God is success.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Among the things that have crept into groups rigidly attached to imitating their shaykhs are partisanship and zeal for their shaykhs’ way, even when truth appears with others—especially among descendants of the righteous. When they see someone upon whom the lights of friendship with God and the secrets of special election have appeared, they wish to turn him from the way of truth back to mingling with people, through envy from within, after truth has become clear. Those turning towards the Real are told: pardon and overlook until truth appears. Do not attend to their contentiousness or occupy yourselves with their faults, lest you become worse than they. A gnostic said: “Never occupy yourself with someone who harms you; occupy yourself with God and He will turn that person from you. Many have erred here: they occupied themselves with those who harmed them, and the harm lasted, accompanied by sin. Had they returned to their Master, He would have sufficed them.” Someone envied or harmed should instead become absent from the envier and his plot and attend to the rights of servanthood and witnessing the grandeur of Lordship. God does not abandon one who takes refuge with Him, nor frustrate the aim of one who relies upon Him. Through God is success."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>Another of the Jews’ empty wishes is their claim to exclusive possession of Paradise, as the Real makes clear:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Another of the Jews’ empty wishes is their claim to exclusive possession of Paradise, as the Real makes clear:"}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C151.1","page":151,"ar":"المدراس - بتقديم الراء على الألف: البيت الذي يدرسون فيه. وقال في النهاية: مفعال غريب في المكان.","en":"Al-midrās—with rāʾ before the alif—is the building in which they study. Al-Nihāya says that mifʿāl is an unusual pattern for a place noun.","anchor":"بيت المدراس"}],"english_characters":3301,"arabic_characters":2604},{"id":"q2-111-112","q":"2:111–112","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:111–112 · Empty claims and genuine surrender","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 152–153","cairo":"I:152–153","source_page_first":152,"source_page_last":153,"ar":"وَقالُوا لَنْ يَدْخُلَ الْجَنَّةَ إِلاَّ مَنْ كانَ هُوداً أَوْ نَصارى تِلْكَ أَمانِيُّهُمْ قُلْ هاتُوا بُرْهانَكُمْ إِنْ كُنْتُمْ صادِقِينَ (111) بَلى مَنْ أَسْلَمَ وَجْهَهُ لِلَّهِ وَهُوَ مُحْسِنٌ فَلَهُ أَجْرُهُ عِنْدَ رَبِّهِ وَلا خَوْفٌ عَلَيْهِمْ وَلا هُمْ يَحْزَنُونَ (112)\nقلت: وَقالُوا عطف على وَدَّ الَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا، والضمير يعود على أهل الكتاب من اليهود والنصارى، أي: وقالت اليهود: لن يدخل الجنة إلا من كان هوداً، وقالت النصارى: لن يدخل الجنة إلا من كان نصارى، وهُوداً: جمع هائد، كبازل وبُزْل وحائل وحول، والأمانى: جمع أمنية، وهي ما يتمنى المرء ويشتهيه، وأصله أمْنُوية كأضْحُوكَة وأعجوبة، فقلبت الواو ياء وأدغمت، وهاتُوا: اسم فعل بمعنى الأمر، ومعناه آتٍ، وأهمل ماضيه ومضارعه، وأَسْلَمَ معناه: استسلم وخضع، والخوف مما يتوقع، والحزن على ما وَقع.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وقالت اليهود: لَنْ يَدْخُلَ الْجَنَّةَ إلا مَن كان يهوديّاً، أي: على دينهم، وقالت النصارى: لن يدخل الجنة إلا مَن كان نصرانيّاً، وهذه دعاوى باطلة، وأماني فارغة ليس عليها بينة، بل مجرد أمانيهم الكاذبة، قُلْ لهم يا محمد: هاتُوا بُرْهانَكُمْ أنكم مختصون بالجنة إِنْ كُنْتُمْ صادِقِينَ في هذه الأمنية، بل يدخلها غيركم من أهل الإسلام والإحسان، فإن مَنْ أَسْلَمَ وَجْهَهُ لِلَّهِ أي: انقاد بكليته إليه وَهُوَ مُحْسِنٌ في أفعاله واعتقاده، فَلَهُ أَجْرُهُ عِنْدَ رَبِّهِ وهو دخول النعيم والنظر إلى وجهه الكريم، وَلا خَوْفٌ عَلَيْهِمْ من مكروه يُتوقع وَلا هُمْ يَحْزَنُونَ على فوات شيء يحتاجون إليه لأنهم في ضيافة الكريم تُساق إليهم المسار وتدفع عنهم المضار، وبالله التوفيق.\nالإشارة: من جملة ما دخل على بعض الفقراء أنهم يَخُصون الخصوصية بهم وبمن تبع شيخهم، وينفونها عن غيرهم، وهذه نزعة يهودية، وتحكم على القدرة الإلهية، فيقال لهم: تلك أمانيكم الفارغة، بل ينالها غيركم، فمن قصد الله صادقاً وجده، وأنجز بالوفاء موعده، فمن خضع لله وانقاد لأولياء الله، فله أجره عند ربه، وهو المعرفة به، ولا خوف عليه من القطيعة، ولا يحزن على فوات نصيبه من المعرفة. وبالله التوفيق، وهو الهادي إلى سواء الطريق.\nولما قدم نصارى نجران على النبي صلى الله عليه وسلّم سمعت بهم اليهود، فجاءوا إليهم، وتناظروا حتى تسابوا، وكفر اليهود بعيسى وبملّته والإنجيل، وكفر النصارى بموسى وبالتوراة، فأنزل الله فى شأنهم:","quran_ar":"وَقالُوا لَنْ يَدْخُلَ الْجَنَّةَ إِلاَّ مَنْ كانَ هُوداً أَوْ نَصارى تِلْكَ أَمانِيُّهُمْ قُلْ هاتُوا بُرْهانَكُمْ إِنْ كُنْتُمْ صادِقِينَ (111) بَلى مَنْ أَسْلَمَ وَجْهَهُ لِلَّهِ وَهُوَ مُحْسِنٌ فَلَهُ أَجْرُهُ عِنْدَ رَبِّهِ وَلا خَوْفٌ عَلَيْهِمْ وَلا هُمْ يَحْزَنُونَ (112)","quran_en":"111. They say, “None will enter the Garden unless they are Jews or Christians.” Those are their wishes. Say, “Bring your proof, if you are truthful.” 112. Rather, whoever surrenders his face to God and acts with excellence shall have his reward with his Lord. No fear shall be upon them, nor shall they grieve.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Two claims compressed into one sentence","html":"<p>I say: “they said” is conjoined to “those who disbelieved wished.” [Translator’s note: Cairo I:152 prints وَدَّ الَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا here; the preceding verse, 2:109, reads “many of the People of the Book wished.” The printed phrase is retained.] The pronoun refers to Jews and Christians: the Jews said that only Jews would enter Paradise, and the Christians that only Christians would enter it. <i>Hūd<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">هود<\/bdi>) is the plural of <i>hāʾid<\/i>, as <i>buzl<\/i> is of <i>bāzil<\/i> and <i>ḥūl<\/i> of <i>ḥāʾil<\/i>. <i>Amānī<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أماني<\/bdi>) is the plural of <i>umniyya<\/i>, what someone wishes for and desires. Its original form is <i>umnūya<\/i>, like <i>uḍḥūka<\/i> and <i>uʿjūba<\/i>: wāw became yāʾ and was assimilated. <i>Hātū<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">هاتوا<\/bdi>) is an imperative verbal expression (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">اسم فعل<\/bdi>): “bring!” Its past and imperfect forms are unused. To <i>aslama<\/i> is to submit and humble oneself. Fear concerns what is anticipated; grief concerns what has occurred.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “they said” is conjoined to “those who disbelieved wished.” [Translator’s note: Cairo I:152 prints وَدَّ الَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا here; the preceding verse, 2:109, reads “many of the People of the Book wished.” The printed phrase is retained.] The pronoun refers to Jews and Christians: the Jews said that only Jews would enter Paradise, and the Christians that only Christians would enter it. Hūd ( هود ) is the plural of hāʾid , as buzl is of bāzil and ḥūl of ḥāʾil . Amānī ( أماني ) is the plural of umniyya , what someone wishes for and desires. Its original form is umnūya , like uḍḥūka and uʿjūba : wāw became yāʾ and was assimilated. Hātū ( هاتوا ) is an imperative verbal expression ( اسم فعل ): “bring!” Its past and imperfect forms are unused. To aslama is to submit and humble oneself. Fear concerns what is anticipated; grief concerns what has occurred."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Surrender of the whole person","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: the Jews claimed that none but a Jew, someone of their religion, would enter Paradise; the Christians made the corresponding claim for a Christian. These are false claims and empty wishes without evidence, merely their lying aspirations. Tell them, Muḥammad: bring your proof of exclusive entitlement to Paradise, if this wish is truthful. Rather, others enter it—people of surrender and excellence (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الإسلام والإحسان<\/bdi>). Whoever surrenders his face to God, yielding to Him in his entirety, and excels in conduct and belief, has his reward with his Lord: entry into bliss and vision of His noble Face. They fear no anticipated harm and grieve for no needed thing missed. As guests of the Generous One, joys are brought to them and harms repelled. Through God is success.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: the Jews claimed that none but a Jew, someone of their religion, would enter Paradise; the Christians made the corresponding claim for a Christian. These are false claims and empty wishes without evidence, merely their lying aspirations. Tell them, Muḥammad: bring your proof of exclusive entitlement to Paradise, if this wish is truthful. Rather, others enter it—people of surrender and excellence ( الإسلام والإحسان ). Whoever surrenders his face to God, yielding to Him in his entirety, and excels in conduct and belief, has his reward with his Lord: entry into bliss and vision of His noble Face. They fear no anticipated harm and grieve for no needed thing missed. As guests of the Generous One, joys are brought to them and harms repelled. Through God is success."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Special election is not a school’s monopoly","html":"<p>Among the errors that have entered some spiritual aspirants is restricting special election to themselves and those following their shaykh, denying it to others. This is a tendency resembling the Jews’ claim and an attempt to dictate to divine power. They are told: those are your empty wishes; others attain it. Whoever sincerely seeks God finds Him, and He fulfils His promise faithfully. Whoever humbles himself to God and submits to His friends has his reward with his Lord—knowledge of Him. He need not fear severance or grieve over missing his portion of gnosis. Through God is success; He guides to the level way.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Among the errors that have entered some spiritual aspirants is restricting special election to themselves and those following their shaykh, denying it to others. This is a tendency resembling the Jews’ claim and an attempt to dictate to divine power. They are told: those are your empty wishes; others attain it. Whoever sincerely seeks God finds Him, and He fulfils His promise faithfully. Whoever humbles himself to God and submits to His friends has his reward with his Lord—knowledge of Him. He need not fear severance or grieve over missing his portion of gnosis. Through God is success; He guides to the level way."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>When the Christians of Najrān came to the Prophet, the Jews heard of them and came. They debated until they exchanged insults: the Jews denied Jesus, his religion and the Gospel; the Christians denied Moses and the Torah. God revealed concerning them:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"When the Christians of Najrān came to the Prophet, the Jews heard of them and came. They debated until they exchanged insults: the Jews denied Jesus, his religion and the Gospel; the Christians denied Moses and the Torah. God revealed concerning them:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":2547,"arabic_characters":2044},{"id":"q2-113","q":"2:113","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:113 · Mutual denigration and the presumption of one’s own sufficiency","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 153–154","cairo":"I:153–154","source_page_first":153,"source_page_last":154,"ar":"وَقالَتِ الْيَهُودُ لَيْسَتِ النَّصارى عَلى شَيْءٍ وَقالَتِ النَّصارى لَيْسَتِ الْيَهُودُ عَلى شَيْءٍ وَهُمْ يَتْلُونَ الْكِتابَ كَذلِكَ قالَ الَّذِينَ لا يَعْلَمُونَ مِثْلَ قَوْلِهِمْ فَاللَّهُ يَحْكُمُ بَيْنَهُمْ يَوْمَ الْقِيامَةِ فِيما كانُوا فِيهِ يَخْتَلِفُونَ (113)\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله إخباراً عن مقالات اليهود والنصارى وتقبيحاً لصنيعهم: وَقالَتِ الْيَهُودُ في الرد على النصارى: لَيْسَتِ النَّصارى عَلى شَيْءٍ يعتد به، وَقالَتِ النَّصارى في سَبِّ اليهود: لَيْسَتِ الْيَهُودُ عَلى شَيْءٍ يعتمد عليه، والحالة أنهم يَتْلُونَ الْكِتابَ، فاليهود يتلون التوراة وفيها البشارة بعيسى عليه السلام، والنصارى يتلون الإنجيل، وفيه تقرير شريعة التوراة وصحة نبوة موسى عليه السلام، فقد كفرت كلُّ فرقة بكتابها غضباً وتعصباً، ومثل مقالتهم هذه قالَ الَّذِينَ لا يَعْلَمُونَ، وهم المشركون، فقالوا: ليس المسلمون على شيء، فَاللَّهُ يَحْكُمُ بَيْنَهُمْ يَوْمَ الْقِيامَةِ فِيما كانُوا فِيهِ يَخْتَلِفُونَ فيدخل أهل الحق الجنة وأهلَ الباطل النار. وبالله التوفيق.\nالإشارة: كل ما قصه الحق تعالى علينا من مساوئ غيرنا فالمقصود به التنفير والتحذير من مثل ما ارتكبوه، والتخلق بضد ما فعلوه، فكل من تراه ينقص الناس ويصغّرهم فهو أصغرهم، وكل من تراه يقول: أصحاب سيدي فلان ليسوا على شيء، وأصحاب سيدي فلان ليس عندهم شيء، فليس هو على شيء، وقد ابتلي بعض المتصوفة بهذا الوصف الذميم، ينصب الميزان على الناس، فيسقط قوما ويرفع آخرين، وهو يتلو كتاب الله، ويسمع قوله تعالى: وَلا تَجَسَّسُوا وَلا يَغْتَبْ بَعْضُكُمْ بَعْضاً ... الآية.\nوأكثر ما تجد هذا الوصف في بعض الفقهاء المتجمدين على ظاهر الشريعة، يعتقد ألا علم فوق علمه، ولا فهم فوق فهمه، كيف؟ والله تعالى يقول: وَما أُوتِيتُمْ مِنَ الْعِلْمِ إِلَّا قَلِيلًا، وَفَوْقَ كُلِّ ذِي عِلْمٍ عَلِيمٌ، وقد قال إمام الحرمين: (لإِنْ أُدْخلَ ألْفَ كَافرٍ في الإسْلامِ بِشُبْهَةٍ خَيْرٌ مِنْ إِخراجِ واحدٍ منْه بشُبْهة) .\nفالواجب على مَن أراد السلامة أن يُحسن الظن بجميع المسلمين، ويعتقد فيهم أنهم كلهم صالحون، ففي الحديث: «خَصْلَتَان لَيْسَ فوقهما شيء من الخير: حُسْن الظنِّ بالله، وحُسْن الظن بعباد الله، وخصلتان ليس فوقهما شيء من الشرِّ: سُوءُ الظن بِالله، وسوء الظن بعباد الله» . وبالله التوفيق.\nثم وبّخ الحق- تعالى- النصارى على منع الناس من بيت المقدس وإيذاء مَن يصلي فيه، وطرح الأقذار فيه، مع زعمهم أنهم على الحق دون غيرهم، قاله ابن عباس، أو كفار قريش حيث منعوا المسلمين من الصلاة فيه، وصدوا رسول الله عن الوصول إليه، قاله ابن زيد، والتحقيق: أن الحق تعالى وبخ الجميع، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَقالَتِ الْيَهُودُ لَيْسَتِ النَّصارى عَلى شَيْءٍ وَقالَتِ النَّصارى لَيْسَتِ الْيَهُودُ عَلى شَيْءٍ وَهُمْ يَتْلُونَ الْكِتابَ كَذلِكَ قالَ الَّذِينَ لا يَعْلَمُونَ مِثْلَ قَوْلِهِمْ فَاللَّهُ يَحْكُمُ بَيْنَهُمْ يَوْمَ الْقِيامَةِ فِيما كانُوا فِيهِ يَخْتَلِفُونَ (113)","quran_en":"113. The Jews say, “The Christians have no foundation,” and the Christians say, “The Jews have no foundation,” although they recite the Book. Those who do not know speak in the same way. God will judge between them on the Day of Resurrection concerning their differences.","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"A dispute that contradicts the books they read","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, reports the Jews’ and Christians’ statements and condemns their conduct. The Jews reply to the Christians, “They stand upon nothing worth considering”; the Christians insult the Jews, “They stand upon nothing reliable.” Yet they recite Scripture: the Jews read the Torah, which foretells Jesus, and the Christians the Gospel, which confirms the Torah’s Law and Moses’ prophethood. Each party thus disbelieves in its own Book through anger and partisanship. Those without knowledge—the polytheists—make the same assertion: the Muslims stand upon nothing. God will judge their disputes on the Day of Resurrection, admitting the people of truth to Paradise and the people of falsehood to the Fire. Through God is success.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, reports the Jews’ and Christians’ statements and condemns their conduct. The Jews reply to the Christians, “They stand upon nothing worth considering”; the Christians insult the Jews, “They stand upon nothing reliable.” Yet they recite Scripture: the Jews read the Torah, which foretells Jesus, and the Christians the Gospel, which confirms the Torah’s Law and Moses’ prophethood. Each party thus disbelieves in its own Book through anger and partisanship. Those without knowledge—the polytheists—make the same assertion: the Muslims stand upon nothing. God will judge their disputes on the Day of Resurrection, admitting the people of truth to Paradise and the people of falsehood to the Fire. Through God is success."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Do not make yourself the measure of everyone","html":"<p>Everything the Real recounts of others’ faults is intended to deter and warn us against committing the same, and to have us cultivate the opposite. Whoever belittles and diminishes others is himself the smallest. Whoever says, “The companions of master so-and-so stand upon nothing; those of master so-and-so possess nothing,” himself stands upon nothing. Some Sufis have been afflicted by this blameworthy trait: setting up a scale over people, bringing some down and raising others, while reading God’s Book and hearing His words, “Do not spy, and do not backbite one another” [49:12].<\/p><p>You find this especially among jurists rigidly fixed upon the outward Sacred Law, imagining no knowledge above theirs and no understanding beyond theirs. How can this be, when God says, “You have been given but little knowledge” [17:85], and “Above every possessor of knowledge is one more knowing” [12:76]? The Imām al-Ḥaramayn said: “To admit a thousand unbelievers into Islam on a doubtful ground is better than to expel one person from it on a doubtful ground.”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-76\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B072]<\/a><\/p><p>Whoever desires safety must think well of all Muslims and believe them all righteous. In the hadith: “Two traits have no goodness above them: thinking well of God and thinking well of God’s servants. Two traits have no evil above them: thinking ill of God and thinking ill of His servants.” Through God is success.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Everything the Real recounts of others’ faults is intended to deter and warn us against committing the same, and to have us cultivate the opposite. Whoever belittles and diminishes others is himself the smallest. Whoever says, “The companions of master so-and-so stand upon nothing; those of master so-and-so possess nothing,” himself stands upon nothing. Some Sufis have been afflicted by this blameworthy trait: setting up a scale over people, bringing some down and raising others, while reading God’s Book and hearing His words, “Do not spy, and do not backbite one another” [49:12]. You find this especially among jurists rigidly fixed upon the outward Sacred Law, imagining no knowledge above theirs and no understanding beyond theirs. How can this be, when God says, “You have been given but little knowledge” [17:85], and “Above every possessor of knowledge is one more knowing” [12:76]? The Imām al-Ḥaramayn said: “To admit a thousand unbelievers into Islam on a doubtful ground is better than to expel one person from it on a doubtful ground.” [B072] Whoever desires safety must think well of all Muslims and believe them all righteous. In the hadith: “Two traits have no goodness above them: thinking well of God and thinking well of God’s servants. Two traits have no evil above them: thinking ill of God and thinking ill of His servants.” Through God is success."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real next reproaches the Christians for preventing people from the sanctuary in Jerusalem, harming those praying there and casting filth into it, while claiming that they alone follow truth: this is Ibn ʿAbbās’s explanation. Or He reproaches the unbelieving Quraysh for preventing Muslims from praying in the sanctuary and barring the Messenger’s access: this is Ibn Zayd’s view. The sound conclusion is that He reproaches them all, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real next reproaches the Christians for preventing people from the sanctuary in Jerusalem, harming those praying there and casting filth into it, while claiming that they alone follow truth: this is Ibn ʿAbbās’s explanation. Or He reproaches the unbelieving Quraysh for preventing Muslims from praying in the sanctuary and barring the Messenger’s access: this is Ibn Zayd’s view. The sound conclusion is that He reproaches them all, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":2572,"arabic_characters":2284},{"id":"q2-114","q":"2:114","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:114 · The sanctuaries of worship and the sanctuaries within","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 154–155","cairo":"I:154–155","source_page_first":154,"source_page_last":155,"ar":"وَمَنْ أَظْلَمُ مِمَّنْ مَنَعَ مَساجِدَ اللَّهِ أَنْ يُذْكَرَ فِيهَا اسْمُهُ وَسَعى فِي خَرابِها أُولئِكَ ما كانَ لَهُمْ أَنْ يَدْخُلُوها إِلاَّ خائِفِينَ لَهُمْ فِي الدُّنْيا خِزْيٌ وَلَهُمْ فِي الْآخِرَةِ عَذابٌ عَظِيمٌ (114)\nقلت: مَنْ مبتدأ، وأَظْلَمُ خبر، وأَنْ يُذْكَرَ إما منصوب على إسقاط الخافض وتسلط الفعل عليه، أي:\nمن أن يذكر، أو بدل اشتمال من مَساجِدَ، أو مجرور بالحرف المحذوف، قاله سيبويه. وخائِفِينَ حال من الواو.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: لا أحَد أكثرُ جُرْماً ولا أعظم ظلماً مِمَّنْ يمنع مَساجِدَ اللَّهِ من أَنْ يُذْكَرَ اسم الله فيها، جماعة أو فرادى، في صلاة أو غيرها، وَسَعى فِي خَرابِها حيث عطل عمارتها، أُولئِكَ ما كانَ ينبغي لَهُمْ أَنْ يَدْخُلُوها إلا بخشية وخشوع، فكيف يجترءون على تخريبها؟ أو ما كان الواجب أن يدخلوها إلا خائفين من المؤمنين أن يبطشوا بهم فضلاً عن أن يمنعوهم منها، أو ما كانَ لَهُمْ في علم الله وقضائه أَنْ يَدْخُلُوها إِلَّا خائِفِينَ، فيكون وعداً أنجزه الله لهم، وقد فتح الله لهم مكة والشام، فكان لا يدخل بيت الله الحرام كافر إلا خفية، خائفاً من القتل، ولا يدخل نصراني بيت المقدس إلا خائفاً من المسلمين، فنالهم فِي الدُّنْيا خِزْيٌ وهو قتل الحربي، وضرب الجزية على الذمي، وخزي المشركين قتلهم يوم الفتح، وإذلالهم بدخولها عليهم عنوة، ولمن مات على الكفر فِي الْآخِرَةِ عَذابٌ عَظِيمٌ.\nوهذه الآية- وإن نزلت في الكفار- فهي عامة لكل من يمنع الناس من الذكر في المساجد، كيفما كان قياماً أو قعوداً، جماعة أو فرادى. والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: مساجدُ الله هي حضرة القلوب وحضرة الأرواح وحضرة الأسرار، فحضرة القلوب لأهل المراقبة من أهل الإيمان، وحضرة الأرواح والأسرار لأهل المشاهدة والمكالمة من أهل الإحسان، فمن منع نفسه من الدخول في هذه الحضرات الثلاث، وسعى في خراب باطنه باتباع الحظوظ والشهوات، ومال إلى الدنيا وزخارفها الغرارات، فلا أحد أظلم منه نفساً، ولا أبخس منه صفقةً. فلا ينجعُ في هؤلاء إلا خوف مُزعج أو شوقٍ مقلق. فإن لم يكن أحد من هذين بقي على غيّه حتى مخايل الموت، فيحن إلى الدخول فيها خائفاً، ولا ينفع حينئذٍ الندم، وقد زلّت به القدم، له في الدنيا ذلك الفقر والجزع، وله في الآخرة غم الحجاب وسوء الحساب وحسرة العتاب، نسأل الله العافية في الدارين. آمين، بمنه وكرمه.\nوقال القشيري: نفْسُ العابدِ وَطَنُ العبادة، وقلب العارف وطن المعرفة، وروح الواجد وطن المحبة، وسر الموحِّد وطن المشاهدة، ولا أظلم ممن سعى في خراب وطن العابد بالشهوات، وفي وطن المعرفة بالمُنى والعلاقات، وفي وطن المحبة بالحظوظ والمساكنات، وفي وطن الموحد بالالتفات إلى القربات. هـ. وبالله التوفيق.\nولمَّا ذكر الحق تعالى تعطيل بعض المساجد والمنع من الصلاة فيها، وسع على عباده فى الصلاة حيث شاءوا، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَمَنْ أَظْلَمُ مِمَّنْ مَنَعَ مَساجِدَ اللَّهِ أَنْ يُذْكَرَ فِيهَا اسْمُهُ وَسَعى فِي خَرابِها أُولئِكَ ما كانَ لَهُمْ أَنْ يَدْخُلُوها إِلاَّ خائِفِينَ لَهُمْ فِي الدُّنْيا خِزْيٌ وَلَهُمْ فِي الْآخِرَةِ عَذابٌ عَظِيمٌ (114)","quran_en":"114. Who is more unjust than one who prevents God’s name from being remembered in His places of worship and strives to ruin them? Such people ought not to enter them except in fear. They have disgrace in this world and a tremendous punishment in the Hereafter.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The construction of prevention","html":"<p>I say: “who” is the subject and “more unjust” its predicate. “That His name should be remembered” is either accusative after the preposition is omitted and the verb governs it directly—“prevents from its being remembered”; or an apposition of inclusion (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">بدل اشتمال<\/bdi>) to “places of worship”; or genitive through the omitted preposition, according to Sībawayh. “In fear” is circumstantial, relating to the plural subject of “enter.”<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “who” is the subject and “more unjust” its predicate. “That His name should be remembered” is either accusative after the preposition is omitted and the verb governs it directly—“prevents from its being remembered”; or an apposition of inclusion ( بدل اشتمال ) to “places of worship”; or genitive through the omitted preposition, according to Sībawayh. “In fear” is circumstantial, relating to the plural subject of “enter.”"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Preventing remembrance in God’s mosques","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: no one commits a greater crime or injustice than one who prevents God’s name from being remembered in His mosques, in groups or individually, in prayer or otherwise, and strives to ruin them by obstructing their flourishing. Such people should enter only with reverence and humility: how dare they destroy them? Alternatively, they ought to enter only fearing that the believers might seize them, far less prevent the believers from entering. Or, in God’s knowledge and decree, they were to enter only in fear: this is then a promise He fulfilled.<\/p><p>God opened Mecca and Syria to the believers. Thereafter no unbeliever entered the Sacred House except secretly, fearing death, and no Christian entered the sanctuary in Jerusalem except in fear of the Muslims. They thus suffered worldly disgrace: the killing of a belligerent unbeliever and the imposition of the poll-tax on a protected non-Muslim. The polytheists’ disgrace was their being killed on the day of the conquest and humiliated by the city’s forcible entry. Whoever died in unbelief has a tremendous punishment in the Hereafter.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-77\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B073]<\/a><\/p><p>Although revealed concerning unbelievers, the verse applies generally to anyone preventing people from remembrance in mosques, however performed: standing or sitting, together or alone. And God Most High knows best.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: no one commits a greater crime or injustice than one who prevents God’s name from being remembered in His mosques, in groups or individually, in prayer or otherwise, and strives to ruin them by obstructing their flourishing. Such people should enter only with reverence and humility: how dare they destroy them? Alternatively, they ought to enter only fearing that the believers might seize them, far less prevent the believers from entering. Or, in God’s knowledge and decree, they were to enter only in fear: this is then a promise He fulfilled. God opened Mecca and Syria to the believers. Thereafter no unbeliever entered the Sacred House except secretly, fearing death, and no Christian entered the sanctuary in Jerusalem except in fear of the Muslims. They thus suffered worldly disgrace: the killing of a belligerent unbeliever and the imposition of the poll-tax on a protected non-Muslim. The polytheists’ disgrace was their being killed on the day of the conquest and humiliated by the city’s forcible entry. Whoever died in unbelief has a tremendous punishment in the Hereafter. [B073] Although revealed concerning unbelievers, the verse applies generally to anyone preventing people from remembrance in mosques, however performed: standing or sitting, together or alone. And God Most High knows best."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The presences of hearts, spirits and secrets","html":"<p>God’s mosques are the presence of hearts, the presence of spirits and the presence of secrets. The presence of hearts belongs to the people of watchfulness among those of faith; the presences of spirits and secrets belong to those of witnessing and divine converse among the people of spiritual excellence. Whoever bars himself from these three presences, strives to ruin his inward being through private portions and desires, and inclines towards the world’s deceptive adornments, is unsurpassed in injustice to himself and loss in his bargain.<\/p><p>Only a fear that shakes him or a longing that unsettles him can benefit such a person. Without either, he persists in error until death’s signs appear. Then he longs to enter, afraid; but remorse no longer helps, and his foot has slipped. In this world he has poverty and panic; in the next, the anguish of veiling, an evil reckoning and the sorrow of reproach. We ask God for well-being in both abodes, through His favour and generosity. Amen.<\/p><p>Al-Qushayrī says: the worshipper’s self is the homeland of worship; the gnostic’s heart, the homeland of knowledge; the ecstatic’s spirit, the homeland of love; and the unifier’s secret, the homeland of witnessing. None is more unjust than one who ruins the worshipper’s homeland through desires, that of knowledge through wishes and attachments, that of love through private portions and settling into repose, and that of the unifier through attending to acts of drawing near themselves. Through God is success.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"God’s mosques are the presence of hearts, the presence of spirits and the presence of secrets. The presence of hearts belongs to the people of watchfulness among those of faith; the presences of spirits and secrets belong to those of witnessing and divine converse among the people of spiritual excellence. Whoever bars himself from these three presences, strives to ruin his inward being through private portions and desires, and inclines towards the world’s deceptive adornments, is unsurpassed in injustice to himself and loss in his bargain. Only a fear that shakes him or a longing that unsettles him can benefit such a person. Without either, he persists in error until death’s signs appear. Then he longs to enter, afraid; but remorse no longer helps, and his foot has slipped. In this world he has poverty and panic; in the next, the anguish of veiling, an evil reckoning and the sorrow of reproach. We ask God for well-being in both abodes, through His favour and generosity. Amen. Al-Qushayrī says: the worshipper’s self is the homeland of worship; the gnostic’s heart, the homeland of knowledge; the ecstatic’s spirit, the homeland of love; and the unifier’s secret, the homeland of witnessing. None is more unjust than one who ruins the worshipper’s homeland through desires, that of knowledge through wishes and attachments, that of love through private portions and settling into repose, and that of the unifier through attending to acts of drawing near themselves. Through God is success."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>Having mentioned the obstruction of some mosques and prevention of prayer there, the Real gives His servants breadth to pray where they may, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Having mentioned the obstruction of some mosques and prevention of prayer there, the Real gives His servants breadth to pray where they may, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":3434,"arabic_characters":2394},{"id":"q2-115","q":"2:115","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:115 · Wherever you turn, there is God’s Face","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 155–157","cairo":"I:155–157","source_page_first":155,"source_page_last":157,"ar":"وَلِلَّهِ الْمَشْرِقُ وَالْمَغْرِبُ فَأَيْنَما تُوَلُّوا فَثَمَّ وَجْهُ اللَّهِ إِنَّ اللَّهَ واسِعٌ عَلِيمٌ (115)\nقلت: (أينما) شرطية، و (تولوا) شَرْطُها، وجملة (فثم) جوابها، و «وَلْي» يستعمل بمعنى أدبر وبمعنى أقبل، تقول: وليت عن كذا أو كذا، والوجه هنا بمعنى الجهة، تقول: سافرت في وجه كذا، أي في جهة كذا. قاله ابن عطية.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَلِلَّهِ الْمَشْرِقُ وَالْمَغْرِبُ، والجهات كلها له، لا يختص ملكه بمكان دون آخر، فإذا مُنعتم من الصلاة في المساجد ففي أي مكان كنتم ووليتم وجهكم إلى القبلة التي أمرتم بالتوجه إليها فثم جهته التي أمر بها، أو فثم ذاته المقدسة، أي: عالم مطلع على ما يفعل فيه، إِنَّ اللَّهَ واسِعٌ بإحاطته بالأشياء، أو برحمته يريد التوسعة على عباده، عَلِيمٌ بمصالحهم وأعمالهم في الأماكن كلها.\nوعن ابن عمر: أنها نزلت في صلاة المسافر على الراحلة حيثما توجهت به، وقيل: في قوم عميت عليهم القبلة فصلوا إلى أنحاء مختلفة، فلما أصبحوا تبينوا خطأهم، وعلى هذا: لو أخطأ المجتهد ثم تبين له الخطأ، لم يلزمه التدارك. قاله البيضاوي.\nالإشارة: اعلم أن الأماكن والجهات، وكل ما ظهر من الكائنات، قائمة بأنوار الصفات، ممحوة بأحدية الذات، «كان الله ولا شيء معه، وهو الآن على ما عليه كان» إذ لا وجود لشئ مع الله، فَأَيْنَما تُوَلُّوا فَثَمَّ\nوَجْهُ اللَّهِ\n، محق الآثار بأفلاك الأنوار، وانمحت الأنوار بأحدية الأسرار، وانفرد بالوجود الواحد القهار، ولله درّ القائل:\nمُذْ عَرفْتُ الإِلَهَ لَمْ أَرَ غَيْراً ... وكَذّا الْغَيْرُ عندنا ممنوع\nمُذْ تَجَمَّعْتْ مَا خَشيتُ افْتِراقًا ... فأَنّا اليَوْمَ وَاصلٌ مجموع\nوقال آخر:\nفالكلُّ دونَ اللهِ إنْ حَققتَهُ ... عَدم عَلَى التفصيلِ والإجمالِ\nمَن لاَ وجُودَ لذاتهِ مِنْ ذاتِهِ ... فوجُودُهُ لولاهُ عينُ مُحَالِ\nوقال صاحب العينية:\nتَجلَّى حَبِيبي فِي مَرَائِي جَمَالِهِ ... فَفِي كُلِّ مَرْئِيّ لِلْحَبِيبِ طَلاَئِعُ\nفَلَمَّا تَبَدَّى حُسْنُه مُتَنوعاً ... تَسمَّى بأسْماءٍ فهُنَّ مَطالِعُ\nوقال الششتري:\nمَحْبوبي قدْ عَمَّ الوجودْ ... وقدْ ظهرَ في بيضٍ وسُودْ\nقال بعض السلف: (دخلت ديرا فجاء وقت الصلاة، فقلت لبعض النصارى: دُلني على بقعة طاهرة أصلي فيها، فقال لي: طهر قلبك عما سواه، وقف حيث شئت، قال: فخَجِلت منه) . ويحكى عن أبى يزيد رضي الله عنه أنه كان يصلي إلى أي جهة شاء، ويتلو هذه الآية، فالوجه عند أهل التحقيق هو عين الذات، يعني أسرار الذات وأنوار الصفات. قال تعالى: كُلُّ شَيْءٍ هالِكٌ إِلَّا وَجْهَهُ أي: كل شيء فانٍ ومستهلك في الحال والاستقبال إلا ذاته المقدسة، وأنشدوا:\nفالعارِفُون فَنَوْا بأَن لَمْ يَشْهَدُوا ... شَيْئاً سِوَى المُتَكَبِّرِ المتَعالِي\nورَأوْا سواهُ عَلَى الحقيقةِ هَالِكاً ... فِي الحالِ وَالْمَاضِي والاستقبال\nوقلت في تائيتي الخمرية في وصف الخمرة الأزلية:\nتنزهت عن حكم الحلول في وصفها ... فليس لها في سوى شَكْلهِ حَلَّتِ\nتجلت عروسا في مرائي جمالها ... وأرخت ستور الكبرياء بِعزَّةِ\nفمَا ظَهَر في الكون غير بهائها ... وما احتجبت إلا لحجب سريرة\nولما قالت اليهود: عزير ابن الله، وقالت النصارى: الْمَسِيحُ ابْنُ الله، وقالت المشركون: الملائكة بنات الله، رد الله تعالى عليهم بقوله:","quran_ar":"وَلِلَّهِ الْمَشْرِقُ وَالْمَغْرِبُ فَأَيْنَما تُوَلُّوا فَثَمَّ وَجْهُ اللَّهِ إِنَّ اللَّهَ واسِعٌ عَلِيمٌ (115)","quran_en":"115. To God belong the east and the west. Wherever you turn, there is God’s Face. God is All-Encompassing, All-Knowing.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Turning and direction","html":"<p>I say: “wherever” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أينما<\/bdi>) is conditional, “you turn” its condition, and “there is…” its response. The verb <i>wallā<\/i> can mean either to turn away or to turn towards: one says <i>wallaytu ʿan kadhā<\/i> or <i>wallaytu kadhā<\/i>. Here <i>wajh<\/i>, “face,” means direction: “I travelled in the direction of such-and-such.” So Ibn ʿAṭiyya explains.<\/p><p class=\"translator-note-ref\">[Translator’s print-source notes: <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/R07-05\">[R07-05]<\/a>]<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “wherever” ( أينما ) is conditional, “you turn” its condition, and “there is…” its response. The verb wallā can mean either to turn away or to turn towards: one says wallaytu ʿan kadhā or wallaytu kadhā . Here wajh , “face,” means direction: “I travelled in the direction of such-and-such.” So Ibn ʿAṭiyya explains. [Translator’s print-source notes: [R07-05] ]"},{"type":"exposition","title":"God’s possession of every direction","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: east and west belong to God, as do all directions. His dominion is not restricted to one place rather than another. If prevented from praying in mosques, wherever you are and turn towards the prayer-direction you have been commanded to face, there is His appointed direction. Or there is His holy Essence: that is, He knows and observes what is done there. God is encompassing in His knowledge of things, or in His mercy, wishing breadth for His servants; He knows their interests and their deeds in every place.<\/p><p>Ibn ʿUmar relates that the verse concerns a traveller’s prayer on his mount, whichever way it turns. Another explanation concerns people unable to determine the prayer-direction, who prayed facing different ways and discovered their mistake in the morning. On that interpretation, one who has exercised judgement and subsequently discovers an error need not make the prayer up. Thus al-Bayḍāwī.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-78\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B074]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: east and west belong to God, as do all directions. His dominion is not restricted to one place rather than another. If prevented from praying in mosques, wherever you are and turn towards the prayer-direction you have been commanded to face, there is His appointed direction. Or there is His holy Essence: that is, He knows and observes what is done there. God is encompassing in His knowledge of things, or in His mercy, wishing breadth for His servants; He knows their interests and their deeds in every place. Ibn ʿUmar relates that the verse concerns a traveller’s prayer on his mount, whichever way it turns. Another explanation concerns people unable to determine the prayer-direction, who prayed facing different ways and discovered their mistake in the morning. On that interpretation, one who has exercised judgement and subsequently discovers an error need not make the prayer up. Thus al-Bayḍāwī. [B074]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The Face, the lights and the secrets","html":"<p>Know that places, directions and every manifested existent stand through the lights of the Attributes and are effaced by the unicity of the Essence (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أحدية الذات<\/bdi>). “God was, and nothing was with Him; He is now as He was.” Nothing has existence alongside God. “Wherever you turn,<\/p><p>there is God’s Face.” The traces are effaced by the orbits of lights, the lights effaced by the unicity of secrets, and the One, the All-Subduing, alone possesses existence. How fine are these words:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">Since knowing God, I have seen no other;<br>“other” is inadmissible with us.<br>Since being gathered, I have feared no parting:<br>today I have arrived, gathered into unity.<\/blockquote><p>Another says:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">All beside God, when truly examined,<br>is nothing, in detail and in sum.<br>What has no existence of itself from itself—<br>its existence, were it not for Him, is sheer impossibility.<\/blockquote><p>The author of the <i>ʿAyniyya<\/i> says:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">My Beloved disclosed Himself in the mirrors of His beauty;<br>in everything seen, the Beloved’s advance signs appear.<br>When His beauty appeared in diverse forms,<br>He took names, and they are its places of rising.<\/blockquote><p>Al-Shushtarī says:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">My Beloved has encompassed existence<br>and appeared in white and black.<\/blockquote><p>One of the predecessors said: “I entered a monastery when prayer-time came and asked a Christian to show me a pure place to pray. He said, ‘Purify your heart of other-than-Him, and stand where you will.’ I was ashamed before him.” It is related that Abū Yazīd used to pray in whatever direction he wished and recite this verse.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-79\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B074]<\/a> For the people of realization, the Face is the Essence itself: the mysteries of the Essence and the lights of the Attributes. God says, “Everything is perishing except His Face” [28:88]: everything is passing away and consumed, now and in the future, except His holy Essence. They recited:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">The gnostics passed away from themselves, seeing nothing<br>but the Supremely Great, the Exalted.<br>They saw all else truly perishing,<br>in present, past and future.<\/blockquote><p>In my <i>Tāʾiyya<\/i> on wine, describing the eternal wine, I said:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">In her description she transcends indwelling;<br>[there is no other in whose form she dwells].<br>A bride, she disclosed herself in mirrors of her beauty,<br>and in might let fall the curtains of majesty.<br>Nothing appears in the cosmos but her splendour;<br>she is hidden only by veils within the heart.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-80\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B075]<\/a><\/blockquote><p class=\"translator-note\">[Translator’s note: Cairo I:157 reads فليس لها في سوى شكله حلت. Ibn ʿAjība quotes the same couplet again at Cairo II:22, on Q5:17, with the order فليس لها سوى في شكله حلت, “there is no other in whose form she dwells.” There the preceding prose expressly explains that the eternal wine is beyond indwelling and union because there is no second entity in which it could dwell or with which it could unite. This establishes the intended sense and pronoun references: “her/she” is the wine, and “his/its form” belongs to the hypothetical other. The bracketed English follows that internal parallel. I:157’s different word order is preserved in the Arabic companion; the full poem’s independent manuscript text has not been established.]<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"Know that places, directions and every manifested existent stand through the lights of the Attributes and are effaced by the unicity of the Essence ( أحدية الذات ). “God was, and nothing was with Him; He is now as He was.” Nothing has existence alongside God. “Wherever you turn, there is God’s Face.” The traces are effaced by the orbits of lights, the lights effaced by the unicity of secrets, and the One, the All-Subduing, alone possesses existence. How fine are these words: Since knowing God, I have seen no other; “other” is inadmissible with us. Since being gathered, I have feared no parting: today I have arrived, gathered into unity. Another says: All beside God, when truly examined, is nothing, in detail and in sum. What has no existence of itself from itself— its existence, were it not for Him, is sheer impossibility. The author of the ʿAyniyya says: My Beloved disclosed Himself in the mirrors of His beauty; in everything seen, the Beloved’s advance signs appear. When His beauty appeared in diverse forms, He took names, and they are its places of rising. Al-Shushtarī says: My Beloved has encompassed existence and appeared in white and black. One of the predecessors said: “I entered a monastery when prayer-time came and asked a Christian to show me a pure place to pray. He said, ‘Purify your heart of other-than-Him, and stand where you will.’ I was ashamed before him.” It is related that Abū Yazīd used to pray in whatever direction he wished and recite this verse. [B074] For the people of realization, the Face is the Essence itself: the mysteries of the Essence and the lights of the Attributes. God says, “Everything is perishing except His Face” [28:88]: everything is passing away and consumed, now and in the future, except His holy Essence. They recited: The gnostics passed away from themselves, seeing nothing but the Supremely Great, the Exalted. They saw all else truly perishing, in present, past and future. In my Tāʾiyya on wine, describing the eternal wine, I said: In her description she transcends indwelling; [there is no other in whose form she dwells]. A bride, she disclosed herself in mirrors of her beauty, and in might let fall the curtains of majesty. Nothing appears in the cosmos but her splendour; she is hidden only by veils within the heart. [B075] [Translator’s note: Cairo I:157 reads فليس لها في سوى شكله حلت. Ibn ʿAjība quotes the same couplet again at Cairo II:22, on Q5:17, with the order فليس لها سوى في شكله حلت, “there is no other in whose form she dwells.” There the preceding prose expressly explains that the eternal wine is beyond indwelling and union because there is no second entity in which it could dwell or with which it could unite. This establishes the intended sense and pronoun references: “her/she” is the wine, and “his/its form” belongs to the hypothetical other. The bracketed English follows that internal parallel. I:157’s different word order is preserved in the Arabic companion; the full poem’s independent manuscript text has not been established.]"},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>When the Jews said, “ʿUzayr is God’s son,” the Christians, “The Messiah is God’s son,” and the polytheists, “The angels are God’s daughters,” God Most High refuted them, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"When the Jews said, “ʿUzayr is God’s son,” the Christians, “The Messiah is God’s son,” and the polytheists, “The angels are God’s daughters,” God Most High refuted them, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C156.1","page":156,"ar":"وهو الشيخ أبو مدين.","en":"He is Shaykh Abū Madyan.","anchor":"وقال آخر: فالكل دون الله إن حققته"},{"id":"C156.2","page":156,"ar":"التوجه نحو البيت الحرام شرط من شروط صحة الصلاة؛ لقوله تعالى: ومن حيث خرجت فول وجهك شطر المسجد الحرام. وأما آية: فأينما تولوا فثم وجه الله، فسبق أنها نزلت في مناسبة مخصوصة؛ وقيل: إنها منسوخة. وقيل: المعنى: أينما كنتم في شرق وغرب فثم وجه الله الذي أمرنا باستقباله وهو الكعبة الشريفة. وما حكي عن أبي يزيد - إن صح - فهو من قبيل الشطحات؛ فلا نأخذ بها.","en":"Facing the Sacred House is one of the conditions for the validity of prayer, because God Most High says, “Wherever you set out from, turn your face towards the Sacred Mosque.” As for the verse “Wherever you turn, there is God’s Face,” it has already been explained that it was revealed on a particular occasion; it has also been said to be abrogated. Another interpretation is: wherever you are, in east or west, there is the direction of God that we have been commanded to face, namely the noble Kaʿba. What is related about Abū Yazīd—if authentic—belongs to the category of ecstatic utterances, and we do not adopt it as a rule.","anchor":"ويحكى عن أبي يزيد ... يصلي إلى أي جهة شاء"}],"english_characters":4538,"arabic_characters":2742},{"id":"q2-116-117","q":"2:116–117","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:116–117 · Origination without dependence and creation subsisting through God","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 157–158","cairo":"I:157–158","source_page_first":157,"source_page_last":158,"ar":"وَقالُوا اتَّخَذَ اللَّهُ وَلَداً سُبْحانَهُ بَلْ لَهُ ما فِي السَّماواتِ وَالْأَرْضِ كُلٌّ لَهُ قانِتُونَ (116) بَدِيعُ السَّماواتِ وَالْأَرْضِ وَإِذا قَضى أَمْراً فَإِنَّما يَقُولُ لَهُ كُنْ فَيَكُونُ (117)\nقلت: هذه الجملة معطوفة على قوله: وَقالَتِ الْيَهُودُ ... الخ، ومن قرأ بغير واو جعلَها مستأنفة، و (بديع) :\nبمعنى مُبدِع، والإبداع: اختراع الشيء من غير تقدم شيء. وقوله: (كن فيكون) ، قدّره سيبويه: فهو يكون، وقرأ ابن عامر بنصب المضارع، ولَحَّنه بعضُهم لأن المنصوبَ في جواب الأمر لا بد أن يصلح جواباً لشرطه، تقول:\nاضرب زيداً فيستقيم، أي: إن تضربه يستقيم، ولا يصلح أن تقول هنا: إن يكن يكن، وقد يجاب بحمله على المعنى، والتقدير: إن قلت كن يكن.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وقالت اليهود والنصارى والمشركون: اتَّخَذَ اللَّهُ وَلَداً تعالى الله عن قولهم، وتنزه عن ذلك لأنه يقتضي الجنسية والمشابهة والاحتياج، والحق منزَّهٌ عن ذلك. بل كل ما استقر في السموات السبع والأرَضين السبع ملكه وعبيده، فكيف يكون العبد ولداً لمالكه؟. وأيضاً كل ما ظهر في الوجود كله قانت، أي: خاضع ومطيع لله، وعابد له، ومقهور تحت حكمه ومشيئته، وذلك مُنافٍ لحال البُنوة.\nوأيضاً: كل ما دخل عالم التكوين فهو مُبْدَع ومَخترَع لله، ومصنوع من مصنوعات الله، فلا يصح أن يكون ولداً، وأيضاً: الولد يحتاج إلى صاحبة ومعالجة ومهلة، والحق تعالى أمره بين الكاف والنون، بل أسرع من لحظ العيون، فإِذا قَضى أَمْراً أي: أراده، فَإِنَّما يَقُولُ لَهُ كُنْ فَيَكُونُ، ولا يتوقف على لفظة كُنْ، وإنما هو كناية عن سرعة الاقتدار.\nقال البيضاوي: واعلم أن السبب في هذه الضلالة أن أرباب الشرائع المتقدمة كانوا يطلقون الأب على الله تعالى، باعتبار أنه السبب الأول، حتى قالوا: إن الأب هو الرب الأصغر، والله تعالى هو الرب الأكبر، ثم ظنّ الجهلة منهم أن المراد به معنى الولادة، فاعتقدوا ذلك تقليداً، ولذلك كفر قائله ومنع منه مطلقاً حسماً لمادة الفساد. هـ.\nالإشارة: اعلم أنك إذا نظرت بعين البصيرة، أو بحق البصيرة، إلى الوجود بأسره، وجدته ذاتاً واحدة، ونسبته من الحق نسبة واحدة، أنوار ظاهرة، وأسرار باطنة، حكمته ظاهرة، وقدرته باطنة حسن ظاهر، ومعنى باطن، عبودية ظاهرة، وأسرار معاني الربوبية باطنة إذ لا قيام للعبودية إلا بأسرار معاني الربوبية، قال تعالى:\nإِنَّ اللَّهَ يُمْسِكُ السَّماواتِ وَالْأَرْضَ أَنْ تَزُولا، وقال تعالى: اللَّهُ نُورُ السَّماواتِ وَالْأَرْضِ، وقال في الحكم: «الأكوان ظاهرها غرة وباطنها عبرة، فالنفس تنظر إلى ظاهر بهجتها، والقلب ينظر إلى باطن عبرتها» . فأهل الفَرْقِ يثبتون الأشياء مستقلة مع الله، وربما تغالى بعضهم فأشركها معه في الألوهية، فتعالى الله عن ذلك علوًّا كبيراً.\nقال محيي الدين الحاتمي: من رأى الخلق لا فعلَ لهم فقد فاز، ومن رآهم لا حياة لهم فقد جاز، ومن رآهم بعين العدم فقد وصل. هـ. قلت: ومن أثبتهم بالله فقد تمكن وصاله، وأنشدوا:\nمَن أبصرَ الخلقَ كالسرابِ ... فقَد تَرقَّى عن الحجابِ\nإِلى وُجودٍ تراهُ رَتْقاً ... بِلاَ ابتعادٍ ولا اقْتِرابِ\nولم تُشَاهِدْ به سواهُ ... هناك تهدى إلى الصوابِ\nفَلا خِطابَ بِه إليهِ ... وَلا مُشِيرَ إلى الخطابِ. هـ.\nولما قال رافع بن حريملة- من أحبار يهود- للرسول صلّى الله عليه وسلّم: أسمعنا كلام الله إن كنت رسوله، أو أرنا آية تصدقك، ردّ الله تعالى عليه، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَقالُوا اتَّخَذَ اللَّهُ وَلَداً سُبْحانَهُ بَلْ لَهُ ما فِي السَّماواتِ وَالْأَرْضِ كُلٌّ لَهُ قانِتُونَ (116) بَدِيعُ السَّماواتِ وَالْأَرْضِ وَإِذا قَضى أَمْراً فَإِنَّما يَقُولُ لَهُ كُنْ فَيَكُونُ (117)","quran_en":"116. They say, “God has taken a child.” Glory to Him! Rather, His is all that is in the heavens and earth; all are obedient to Him. 117. Originator of the heavens and earth: when He decrees a thing, He only says to it, “Be,” and it is.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The response to the command “Be”","html":"<p>I say: this sentence is conjoined to “The Jews said…” [2:113]. In the reading without wāw, it begins independently. <i>Badīʿ<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">بديع<\/bdi>) means <i>mubdiʿ<\/i>, an originator; origination (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الإبداع<\/bdi>) is bringing something into being without anything preceding it. Sībawayh understands “Be, and it is” as “and so it is.” Ibn ʿĀmir reads the imperfect verb in the subjunctive (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">النصب<\/bdi>). Some declared his reading grammatically faulty: a subjunctive answering a command must also work as the response to its conditional equivalent. “Strike Zayd, that he may become upright” means “if you strike him, he becomes upright,” whereas “if it is, it is” does not work here. One may reply by considering the meaning: “If I say ‘Be,’ it is.”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-81\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B076]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: this sentence is conjoined to “The Jews said…” [2:113]. In the reading without wāw, it begins independently. Badīʿ ( بديع ) means mubdiʿ , an originator; origination ( الإبداع ) is bringing something into being without anything preceding it. Sībawayh understands “Be, and it is” as “and so it is.” Ibn ʿĀmir reads the imperfect verb in the subjunctive ( النصب ). Some declared his reading grammatically faulty: a subjunctive answering a command must also work as the response to its conditional equivalent. “Strike Zayd, that he may become upright” means “if you strike him, he becomes upright,” whereas “if it is, it is” does not work here. One may reply by considering the meaning: “If I say ‘Be,’ it is.” [B076]"},{"type":"theology","title":"Why divine sonship is denied","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: the Jews, Christians and polytheists claimed that God took a child. Exalted is He beyond their assertion. Sonship implies a common kind, resemblance and need; the Real transcends these. Everything in the seven heavens and seven earths is His possession and servant: how could a servant be his owner’s child? Everything manifest in existence is also obedient—submissive, compliant and worshipping God, constrained by His decree and will. This is incompatible with sonship.<\/p><p>Everything entering the world of formation (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التكوين<\/bdi>) is originated, invented and made by God; it cannot therefore be His child. A child also requires a consort, a process and a period of time. The Real’s command is between kāf and nūn, the two letters of “Be”—indeed, swifter than the blink of an eye. When He decrees, that is wills, a thing, He only says “Be,” and it is. This does not depend on uttering the word “Be”; the expression alludes to the swiftness of His power.<\/p><p>Al-Bayḍāwī says: the cause of this error was that adherents of earlier revealed Laws used “Father” of God as the first cause, even saying that a father was the lesser lord and God the greater Lord. The ignorant then supposed that literal generation was intended and adopted that belief through imitation. For that reason one who says it is judged unbelieving, and the expression is prohibited altogether to close the source of corruption. Thus ends his explanation.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: the Jews, Christians and polytheists claimed that God took a child. Exalted is He beyond their assertion. Sonship implies a common kind, resemblance and need; the Real transcends these. Everything in the seven heavens and seven earths is His possession and servant: how could a servant be his owner’s child? Everything manifest in existence is also obedient—submissive, compliant and worshipping God, constrained by His decree and will. This is incompatible with sonship. Everything entering the world of formation ( التكوين ) is originated, invented and made by God; it cannot therefore be His child. A child also requires a consort, a process and a period of time. The Real’s command is between kāf and nūn, the two letters of “Be”—indeed, swifter than the blink of an eye. When He decrees, that is wills, a thing, He only says “Be,” and it is. This does not depend on uttering the word “Be”; the expression alludes to the swiftness of His power. Al-Bayḍāwī says: the cause of this error was that adherents of earlier revealed Laws used “Father” of God as the first cause, even saying that a father was the lesser lord and God the greater Lord. The ignorant then supposed that literal generation was intended and adopted that belief through imitation. For that reason one who says it is judged unbelieving, and the expression is prohibited altogether to close the source of corruption. Thus ends his explanation."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Effacement followed by affirmation through God","html":"<p>Know that when you regard all existence with the eye of insight (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عين البصيرة<\/bdi>), or with the truth of insight (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حق البصيرة<\/bdi>), you find it one reality (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ذاتاً واحدة<\/bdi>), standing in one relation to the Real: outward lights and inward secrets; His wisdom outward and His power inward; outward beauty and inward meaning; outward servanthood and the secrets of Lordship’s meanings inward. Servanthood stands only through the secrets of Lordship’s meanings.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-82\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B077]<\/a> God says, “God holds the heavens and earth lest they pass away” [35:41], and “God is the Light of the heavens and earth” [24:35]. In the <i>Ḥikam<\/i>: “Created things are outwardly a delusion, inwardly a lesson. The lower self looks at their outward splendour; the heart looks at their inward lesson.”<\/p><p>The people of separation (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الفرق<\/bdi>) affirm things as independent alongside God. Some go so far as to associate them with Him in divinity. Exalted is God far beyond that. Muḥyī al-Dīn al-Ḥātimī says: whoever sees creatures as having no action has succeeded; whoever sees them as having no life has crossed over; whoever sees them with the eye of non-existence has arrived. I say: whoever affirms them through God has become firmly established in his arrival. They recited:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">Whoever sees creation like a mirage<br>has risen beyond the veil,<br>to existence seen as undivided,<br>without distance or proximity.<br>There you witness none but Him;<br>there you are guided to what is right.<br>No address is directed there to Him,<br>nor any pointer to that address.<\/blockquote>","authorial":true,"text":"Know that when you regard all existence with the eye of insight ( عين البصيرة ), or with the truth of insight ( حق البصيرة ), you find it one reality ( ذاتاً واحدة ), standing in one relation to the Real: outward lights and inward secrets; His wisdom outward and His power inward; outward beauty and inward meaning; outward servanthood and the secrets of Lordship’s meanings inward. Servanthood stands only through the secrets of Lordship’s meanings. [B077] God says, “God holds the heavens and earth lest they pass away” [35:41], and “God is the Light of the heavens and earth” [24:35]. In the Ḥikam : “Created things are outwardly a delusion, inwardly a lesson. The lower self looks at their outward splendour; the heart looks at their inward lesson.” The people of separation ( الفرق ) affirm things as independent alongside God. Some go so far as to associate them with Him in divinity. Exalted is God far beyond that. Muḥyī al-Dīn al-Ḥātimī says: whoever sees creatures as having no action has succeeded; whoever sees them as having no life has crossed over; whoever sees them with the eye of non-existence has arrived. I say: whoever affirms them through God has become firmly established in his arrival. They recited: Whoever sees creation like a mirage has risen beyond the veil, to existence seen as undivided, without distance or proximity. There you witness none but Him; there you are guided to what is right. No address is directed there to Him, nor any pointer to that address."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>When Rāfiʿ ibn Ḥuraymila, one of the Jewish learned men, told the Messenger, “Let us hear God’s speech if you are His messenger, or show us a sign confirming you,” God replied:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"When Rāfiʿ ibn Ḥuraymila, one of the Jewish learned men, told the Messenger, “Let us hear God’s speech if you are His messenger, or show us a sign confirming you,” God replied:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":3843,"arabic_characters":2849},{"id":"q2-118-119","q":"2:118–119","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:118–119 · Signs, certainty and the opening of the heart’s hearing","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 158–160","cairo":"I:158–160","source_page_first":158,"source_page_last":160,"ar":"وَقالَ الَّذِينَ لا يَعْلَمُونَ لَوْلا يُكَلِّمُنَا اللَّهُ أَوْ تَأْتِينا آيَةٌ كَذلِكَ قالَ الَّذِينَ مِنْ قَبْلِهِمْ مِثْلَ قَوْلِهِمْ تَشابَهَتْ قُلُوبُهُمْ قَدْ بَيَّنَّا الْآياتِ لِقَوْمٍ يُوقِنُونَ (118) إِنَّا أَرْسَلْناكَ بِالْحَقِّ بَشِيراً وَنَذِيراً وَلا تَسْأَلْ عَنْ أَصْحابِ الْجَحِيمِ (119)\nقلت: هذه المقالة صدرت من بعض اليهود والمشركين، قالوا ذلك تعنتاً وعناداً، لا طلباً لليقين، فلذلك نفى الله عنهم العلم رأساً، والمقصود في هذه الآيات كلها توبيخ اليهود.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَقالَ الَّذِينَ لا علم عندهم: هلا يُكَلِّمُنَا اللَّهُ حتى نسمع منه أنك رسوله، أَوْ تَأْتِينا آيَةٌ ظاهرة، نراها جهرة تدل على رسالتك، كما كانت لموسى- عليه السلام-.\nوهذه المقالة التي صدرت من اليهود، تَعنتاً وعناداً، قد صدرت ممن قبلهم من أسلافهم، فقالوا: أَرِنَا اللَّهَ جَهْرَةً، ومن النصارى فقالوا: هَلْ يَسْتَطِيعُ رَبُّكَ أَنْ يُنَزِّلَ عَلَيْنا مائِدَةً مِنَ السَّماءِ، ومن المشركين فقالوا:\nلَنْ نُؤْمِنَ لَكَ حَتَّى تَفْجُرَ لَنا مِنَ الْأَرْضِ يَنْبُوعاً، أَوْ تَكُونَ لَكَ جَنَّةٌ مِنْ نَخِيلٍ وَعِنَبٍ فَتُفَجِّرَ الْأَنْهارَ خِلالَها تَفْجِيراً الآية. فقد تماثلت قلوبهم في الكفر والعناد، وتشابهت في العتو والفساد، قد أوضحنا لك الآيات البينات، تحقق رسالتك وتقرر اصطفائيتك، لمن طلب مزيد الإيقان، وكشف البيان على نعت العيان، فأعظمُها القرآن، ثم ما أوضحته من شرائع الأحكام، وما بينته من الحلال والحرام، ثم ما أخبرت به من الغيوب، وما كشفته عن القلوب من الكروب، ثم نطق الجمادات والأحجار، كحنين الجذع وانقياد الأشجار، وتسبيح الحصى، وتسليم الحجر، وقد نبع الماء من بين أصابعه وانهمر، إلى ما لا يعد ولا يحصى.\nفقد أَرْسَلْناكَ بِالْحَقِّ، أي: متلبساً بالحق ومبيناً له، بَشِيراً لمن صدقك واتبعك بالنعيم المقيم، ونَذِيراً لمن خالفك بعذاب الجحيم. فلا تسأل عن حالهم إذا أفضوا إليه، فإنه أعظم من أن يذكر، وأفظع من أن يسمع، إذ لا يمكن تفسير حالهم، ولا يستطيع أحد سماع أهوالهم، فالله يعصمنا من موارد الردى، ويوفقنا لاتباع الحق والهدى، أو لا يسألك ربك عنهم فهو أعلم بحالهم، وبالله التوفيق.\nالإشارة: طلب الكرامات وظهور الآيات من طبع أهل الجهل والعناد، وليس هو من شيم أهل الهداية والاسترشاد. فالطريق واضح لمن طلب السبيل، والحق لائح لمن أبصر الدليل، فمن كَحل عين بصيرته بإثمد التوحيد الخاص، لم يقع بصره إلا على الحق، ولا يعرف إلا إياه، ورأى الأشياء كلها قائمة بالله، بل لا وجود لها مع الله، ومن فتح الله سمع قلبه لم يسمع إلا من الحق، ولا يسمع إلا به، كما قال القائل: أنا بالله أنطق ومن الله أسمع.\nوقال الجنيد رضي الله عنه: (لي أربعون سنة أُناجي الحق، والناس يَروْن أني أناجى الخلق) . فالخلق محذوفون عند أهل العلم بالتحقيق، مُثْبَتُونَ عند أهل الجهل والتفريق. يقولون: لولا يكلمنا الله أو تأتينا آية، مع أنه يكلمهم في كل\nوقت وساعة، كذلك قال مَن شاركهم في الجهل بالله، مع وضوح الآيات لمن عرف الله. والله يقول الحق وهو يهدي السبيل.\nولمَّا قالت اليهود والنصارى لرسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلّم: اجعل بيننا وبينك هُدْنة نتبعك بعدها، وأضمَرُوا في نفوسهم أنهم لا يتبعونه حتى يتبع ملتهم، فضحهم الله تعالى، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَقالَ الَّذِينَ لا يَعْلَمُونَ لَوْلا يُكَلِّمُنَا اللَّهُ أَوْ تَأْتِينا آيَةٌ كَذلِكَ قالَ الَّذِينَ مِنْ قَبْلِهِمْ مِثْلَ قَوْلِهِمْ تَشابَهَتْ قُلُوبُهُمْ قَدْ بَيَّنَّا الْآياتِ لِقَوْمٍ يُوقِنُونَ (118) إِنَّا أَرْسَلْناكَ بِالْحَقِّ بَشِيراً وَنَذِيراً وَلا تَسْأَلْ عَنْ أَصْحابِ الْجَحِيمِ (119)","quran_en":"118. Those who do not know say, “Why does God not speak to us, or a sign come to us?” Those before them spoke in the same way; their hearts resemble one another. We have made the signs clear for people who possess certainty. 119. We have sent you with the truth, as a bearer of good tidings and a warner. Do not ask about the companions of the blazing Fire.","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Obstinacy is not a search for certainty","html":"<p>I say: this statement came from some Jews and polytheists through obstinacy and resistance, not a search for certainty. God therefore denies them knowledge altogether. All these verses aim to reproach the Jews.<\/p><p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: those without knowledge ask why God does not speak to them, so that they may hear from Him that you are His messenger, or why a manifest sign does not come, visible openly, indicating your messengership as Moses had. The Jews’ obstinate statement had precedents: their forefathers said, “Show us God openly” [4:153]; Christians said, “Can your Lord send down a table from heaven for us?” [5:112]; and polytheists said, “We will not believe you until you make a spring burst from the earth for us, or possess a garden of palms and vines and make rivers flow abundantly through it” [17:90–91]. Their hearts are alike in unbelief and resistance, comparable in insolence and corruption.<\/p><p>We have made clear the signs establishing your messengership and election for those seeking increased certainty and disclosure in direct vision. The greatest sign is the Qurʾān; then the ordinances you have clarified and the lawful and forbidden you have explained; then the unseen matters you have reported and the distresses you have lifted from hearts; then the speech of inanimate things and stones, the palm-trunk’s yearning, trees’ obedience, pebbles’ glorification and the stone’s greeting. Water also sprang abundantly from between his fingers, besides countless other signs.<\/p><p>We sent you with the truth, clothed in it and making it clear, bringing the abiding bliss as good news to those who affirm and follow you, and warning opponents of the punishment of the Fire. Do not ask about their condition when they reach it: it is too great to recount, too dreadful to hear. Their state cannot be explained, nor can anyone bear to hear its terrors. May God preserve us from ruin and enable us to follow truth and guidance. Alternatively, your Lord will not question you about them: He knows their condition best.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-83\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B078]<\/a> Through God is success.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: this statement came from some Jews and polytheists through obstinacy and resistance, not a search for certainty. God therefore denies them knowledge altogether. All these verses aim to reproach the Jews. The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: those without knowledge ask why God does not speak to them, so that they may hear from Him that you are His messenger, or why a manifest sign does not come, visible openly, indicating your messengership as Moses had. The Jews’ obstinate statement had precedents: their forefathers said, “Show us God openly” [4:153]; Christians said, “Can your Lord send down a table from heaven for us?” [5:112]; and polytheists said, “We will not believe you until you make a spring burst from the earth for us, or possess a garden of palms and vines and make rivers flow abundantly through it” [17:90–91]. Their hearts are alike in unbelief and resistance, comparable in insolence and corruption. We have made clear the signs establishing your messengership and election for those seeking increased certainty and disclosure in direct vision. The greatest sign is the Qurʾān; then the ordinances you have clarified and the lawful and forbidden you have explained; then the unseen matters you have reported and the distresses you have lifted from hearts; then the speech of inanimate things and stones, the palm-trunk’s yearning, trees’ obedience, pebbles’ glorification and the stone’s greeting. Water also sprang abundantly from between his fingers, besides countless other signs. We sent you with the truth, clothed in it and making it clear, bringing the abiding bliss as good news to those who affirm and follow you, and warning opponents of the punishment of the Fire. Do not ask about their condition when they reach it: it is too great to recount, too dreadful to hear. Their state cannot be explained, nor can anyone bear to hear its terrors. May God preserve us from ruin and enable us to follow truth and guidance. Alternatively, your Lord will not question you about them: He knows their condition best. [B078] Through God is success."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Hearing from the Real","html":"<p>Demanding wonders and manifest signs belongs to the disposition of the ignorant and obstinate, not to those seeking guidance and direction. The road is clear for one who seeks the way, and truth evident for one who perceives its indication. Whoever anoints his inner eye with the antimony of the special realization of unity (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التوحيد الخاص<\/bdi>) sees none but the Real and knows none but Him. He sees all things standing through God—indeed, having no existence alongside Him. Whoever has the hearing of his heart opened by God hears only from the Real and only through Him. As someone said, “Through God I speak; from God I hear.”<\/p><p>Al-Junayd said: “For forty years I have conversed intimately with the Real, while people suppose I converse with creation.” Creation is effaced for those possessing realized knowledge, but affirmed as separate by the ignorant and divisive. They ask why God does not speak to them or a sign come,<\/p><p>although He speaks to them at every time and hour. Those sharing their ignorance of God say the same, despite the clarity of the signs to those who know Him. God speaks the truth and guides to the way.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Demanding wonders and manifest signs belongs to the disposition of the ignorant and obstinate, not to those seeking guidance and direction. The road is clear for one who seeks the way, and truth evident for one who perceives its indication. Whoever anoints his inner eye with the antimony of the special realization of unity ( التوحيد الخاص ) sees none but the Real and knows none but Him. He sees all things standing through God—indeed, having no existence alongside Him. Whoever has the hearing of his heart opened by God hears only from the Real and only through Him. As someone said, “Through God I speak; from God I hear.” Al-Junayd said: “For forty years I have conversed intimately with the Real, while people suppose I converse with creation.” Creation is effaced for those possessing realized knowledge, but affirmed as separate by the ignorant and divisive. They ask why God does not speak to them or a sign come, although He speaks to them at every time and hour. Those sharing their ignorance of God say the same, despite the clarity of the signs to those who know Him. God speaks the truth and guides to the way."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>When Jews and Christians told the Messenger, “Make a truce between us and yourself, after which we will follow you,” while concealing the intention not to follow him until he followed their religion, God exposed them, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"When Jews and Christians told the Messenger, “Make a truce between us and yourself, after which we will follow you,” while concealing the intention not to follow him until he followed their religion, God exposed them, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":3432,"arabic_characters":2780},{"id":"q2-120","q":"2:120","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:120 · Seeking approval from people","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 160–161","cairo":"I:160–161","source_page_first":160,"source_page_last":161,"ar":"وَلَنْ تَرْضى عَنْكَ الْيَهُودُ وَلا النَّصارى حَتَّى تَتَّبِعَ مِلَّتَهُمْ قُلْ إِنَّ هُدَى اللَّهِ هُوَ الْهُدى وَلَئِنِ اتَّبَعْتَ أَهْواءَهُمْ بَعْدَ الَّذِي جاءَكَ مِنَ الْعِلْمِ ما لَكَ مِنَ اللَّهِ مِنْ وَلِيٍّ وَلا نَصِيرٍ (120)\nقلت: الملّة هي الشريعة، وهي ما شرع الله على لسان أنبيائه ورسله، من أمللت الكتاب وأمليته، إذا قرأته.\nوالهوى: رأى يتبع الشهوة.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله لرسوله صلّى الله عليه وسلّم: وَلَنْ تَرْضى عَنْكَ الْيَهُودُ وتتبع دينك أبداً، وَلَا النَّصارى كذلك حَتَّى تَتَّبِعَ مِلَّتَهُمْ على فرض المحال، والمقصود قطعُ رجائه من إسلامهم باختيارهم لأن اتباعه ملتهم محال، وكذلك إسلامهم. ولعله في قوم مخصوصين. ثم زاد في التنفير من اتباعهم فقال: وَلَئِنِ اتَّبَعْتَ أَهْواءَهُمْ الباطلة فرضاً وتقديراً بَعْدَ الَّذِي جاءَكَ مِنَ الْعِلْمِ بالله وبأحكامه على المنهاج القويم، ما لَكَ مِنَ اللَّهِ مِنْ وَلِيٍّ يمنعك منا، وَلا نَصِيرٍ ينصرك من غيرنا، أي: لا ولي ولا نصير لك إلا نحن حيث واليتنا، وأحببتنا، وأظهرت ملتنا، فنحن لك على ما تحب وترضى.\nالإشارة: التماس رضى الناس من علامة الإفلاس، ولن يرضى عنك الناس حتى تتبع أهواءهم، ولئن اتبعت أهواءهم بعد ما تحققت ما هم فيه، إنك إذاً لمن الظالمين، فمن التمس رضى الناس وقع في سخط الله، ومن التمس رضى الله قطع يأسه من الناس. ولذلك قال بعضهم: كل ما سقط من عين الخلق عظم في عين الحق، وكل ما عظم في عين الخلق سقط من عين الحق، وقال آخر: إن الذي تكرهون مني هو الذي يشتهيه قلبي. هـ.\nوقال بعض الصالحين: (لقيتُ بعض الأبدال، فقلت له: دُلَّني على الطريق؟ فقال: لا تخالط الناس فإن مخالطتهم ظلمة، فقلت: لا بد من مخالطتهم وأنا بين أظهرهم؟ فقال لا تعاملهم، فإن معاملتهم خسران. قلت: لا بد من معاملتهم؟ فقال: لا تركن إليهم، فإن في الركون إليهم هلكة، فقلت: هذا لعله يكون؟ فقال: يا هذا، أتخالط البطالين، وتعامل الجاهلين، وتركن إلى الهلكى، وتحب أن يكون قلبك مع الله؟ هيهات.. هذا لا يكون أبدًا، ثم غاب عني ولم أره) .\nولما عاتب الله بني إسرائيل ووبخهم استثنى من آمن منهم، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَلَنْ تَرْضى عَنْكَ الْيَهُودُ وَلا النَّصارى حَتَّى تَتَّبِعَ مِلَّتَهُمْ قُلْ إِنَّ هُدَى اللَّهِ هُوَ الْهُدى وَلَئِنِ اتَّبَعْتَ أَهْواءَهُمْ بَعْدَ الَّذِي جاءَكَ مِنَ الْعِلْمِ ما لَكَ مِنَ اللَّهِ مِنْ وَلِيٍّ وَلا نَصِيرٍ (120)","quran_en":"120. The Jews and Christians will not be pleased with you until you follow their religion. Say, “God’s guidance is the guidance.” Were you to follow their desires after the knowledge that has come to you, you would have neither protector nor helper against God.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Religion and desire","html":"<p>I say: <i>milla<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ملّة<\/bdi>) is a revealed Law, what God ordains upon the tongues of His prophets and messengers. It derives from <i>amlaltu al-kitāba<\/i> or <i>amlaytuhu<\/i>: I recited the written text. Desire (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الهوى<\/bdi>) is an opinion that follows appetite.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: milla ( ملّة ) is a revealed Law, what God ordains upon the tongues of His prophets and messengers. It derives from amlaltu al-kitāba or amlaytuhu : I recited the written text. Desire ( الهوى ) is an opinion that follows appetite."},{"type":"exposition","title":"An impossible hypothesis and a particular group","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, tells His Messenger: the Jews will never be pleased with you and follow your religion, nor the Christians, until you follow theirs—on the supposition of an impossibility. The aim is to cut off his hope that they will embrace Islam of their own choice: as his following their religion is impossible, so too is their conversion. Perhaps this concerns a particular group. He further deters following them: were you, hypothetically, to follow their false desires after receiving knowledge of God and His judgements upon the straight method, you would have no protector against Us and no helper from any other. You have no protector or helper but Us, because you have befriended and loved Us and manifested Our religion; We are therefore for you as you love and approve.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, tells His Messenger: the Jews will never be pleased with you and follow your religion, nor the Christians, until you follow theirs—on the supposition of an impossibility. The aim is to cut off his hope that they will embrace Islam of their own choice: as his following their religion is impossible, so too is their conversion. Perhaps this concerns a particular group. He further deters following them: were you, hypothetically, to follow their false desires after receiving knowledge of God and His judgements upon the straight method, you would have no protector against Us and no helper from any other. You have no protector or helper but Us, because you have befriended and loved Us and manifested Our religion; We are therefore for you as you love and approve."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Approval as a form of dependence","html":"<p>Seeking people’s approval is a sign of spiritual bankruptcy. People will not be pleased with you until you follow their desires; were you to follow them after recognizing their condition, you would be among the wrongdoers. Whoever seeks people’s approval falls into God’s displeasure; whoever seeks God’s approval cuts off expectation from people. Hence someone said: whatever falls in creation’s estimation rises in the Real’s estimation, and whatever rises in creation’s estimation falls in the Real’s. Another said, “What you dislike in me is what my heart desires.”<\/p><p>A righteous man said: “I met one of the substitute saints and asked him to show me the way. He replied, ‘Do not mingle with people: mingling with them is darkness.’ I said, ‘I must mingle with them, since I live among them.’ He said, ‘Do not transact with them: dealing with them is loss.’ I said, ‘I must deal with them.’ He replied, ‘Do not rely upon them: such reliance is ruin.’ I said, ‘Perhaps that is possible.’ He answered, ‘Man, you mingle with the idle, deal with the ignorant, rely on the ruined, and wish your heart to be with God? Far from it: that can never be.’ Then he disappeared from me, and I never saw him again.”<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Seeking people’s approval is a sign of spiritual bankruptcy. People will not be pleased with you until you follow their desires; were you to follow them after recognizing their condition, you would be among the wrongdoers. Whoever seeks people’s approval falls into God’s displeasure; whoever seeks God’s approval cuts off expectation from people. Hence someone said: whatever falls in creation’s estimation rises in the Real’s estimation, and whatever rises in creation’s estimation falls in the Real’s. Another said, “What you dislike in me is what my heart desires.” A righteous man said: “I met one of the substitute saints and asked him to show me the way. He replied, ‘Do not mingle with people: mingling with them is darkness.’ I said, ‘I must mingle with them, since I live among them.’ He said, ‘Do not transact with them: dealing with them is loss.’ I said, ‘I must deal with them.’ He replied, ‘Do not rely upon them: such reliance is ruin.’ I said, ‘Perhaps that is possible.’ He answered, ‘Man, you mingle with the idle, deal with the ignorant, rely on the ruined, and wish your heart to be with God? Far from it: that can never be.’ Then he disappeared from me, and I never saw him again.”"},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>After reproaching and admonishing the Children of Israel, God excepts those among them who believe, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"After reproaching and admonishing the Children of Israel, God excepts those among them who believe, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":2347,"arabic_characters":1812},{"id":"q2-121","q":"2:121","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:121 · Reading the Book as it should be read","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 161–161","cairo":"I:161","source_page_first":161,"source_page_last":161,"ar":"الَّذِينَ آتَيْناهُمُ الْكِتابَ يَتْلُونَهُ حَقَّ تِلاوَتِهِ أُولئِكَ يُؤْمِنُونَ بِهِ وَمَنْ يَكْفُرْ بِهِ فَأُولئِكَ هُمُ الْخاسِرُونَ (121)\nقلت: جملة (يتلونه) حال، و (أولئك) خبر الموصول.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: الَّذِينَ آتَيْناهُمُ الْكِتابَ، كعبد الله بن سلام وأصحابه، حالتهم يَتْلُونَهُ حَقَّ تِلاوَتِهِ غير محرّفين له، ولا كاتمين ما فيه، أُولئِكَ هم الذين يُؤْمِنُونَ بِهِ حقيقة، وأما غيرهم ممن حرف وكتم صفة الرسول صلّى الله عليه وسلّم فقد كفر به، وَمَنْ يَكْفُرْ بِهِ فَأُولئِكَ هُمُ الْخاسِرُونَ أي: الكاملون في الخسران، حيث بخسوا أنفسهم من عَزِّ الدارين.\nالإشارة: ما قيل في التوراة وأصحابه يقال مثله في القرآن وأهله فمن آتاه الله القرآن، وتلاه حق تلاوته، بحيث جَوَّدَ حروفه وتَدبَّر معانيه، وعمل بما فيه، فأولئك هم المؤمنون به حقا، والفائزون بثمار معانيه حلاوة وذوقاً، ومن ترك التدبر في معانيه فقد حرم نفسه ثمار حلاوته، وذلك عين الخسران عند أهل الإيقان. وبالله التوفيق.\nثم رجع الحقّ تعالى إلى تذكيرهم بالنعم، فقال:","quran_ar":"الَّذِينَ آتَيْناهُمُ الْكِتابَ يَتْلُونَهُ حَقَّ تِلاوَتِهِ أُولئِكَ يُؤْمِنُونَ بِهِ وَمَنْ يَكْفُرْ بِهِ فَأُولئِكَ هُمُ الْخاسِرُونَ (121)","quran_en":"121. Those to whom We have given the Book, who recite it as it should be recited—they believe in it. Whoever disbelieves in it, those are the losers.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"A circumstantial clause and the delayed predicate","html":"<p>I say: “they recite it” is a circumstantial clause, and “those…” is the predicate of the relative expression.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “they recite it” is a circumstantial clause, and “those…” is the predicate of the relative expression."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Recitation without concealment","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: those to whom We gave the Book, such as ʿAbd Allāh ibn Salām and his companions, recite it properly, neither distorting it nor concealing its contents. They truly believe it. Those who alter and conceal the Messenger’s description have disbelieved it; whoever disbelieves it is utterly a loser, having deprived himself of honour in both abodes.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: those to whom We gave the Book, such as ʿAbd Allāh ibn Salām and his companions, recite it properly, neither distorting it nor concealing its contents. They truly believe it. Those who alter and conceal the Messenger’s description have disbelieved it; whoever disbelieves it is utterly a loser, having deprived himself of honour in both abodes."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The fruits of attentive recitation","html":"<p>What is said of the Torah and its people applies correspondingly to the Qurʾān and its people. Those to whom God gives the Qurʾān, who recite it properly—perfecting its letters, contemplating its meanings and acting upon it—truly believe it and win the fruits of its meanings in sweetness and taste. Whoever abandons contemplation of its meanings deprives himself of the fruits of its sweetness. For the people of certainty, that is loss itself. Through God is success.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"What is said of the Torah and its people applies correspondingly to the Qurʾān and its people. Those to whom God gives the Qurʾān, who recite it properly—perfecting its letters, contemplating its meanings and acting upon it—truly believe it and win the fruits of its meanings in sweetness and taste. Whoever abandons contemplation of its meanings deprives himself of the fruits of its sweetness. For the people of certainty, that is loss itself. Through God is success."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real returns to reminding them of His blessings, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real returns to reminding them of His blessings, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":1023,"arabic_characters":921},{"id":"q2-122-123","q":"2:122–123","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:122–123 · Gratitude for favour and fear of the final day","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 161–162","cairo":"I:161–162","source_page_first":161,"source_page_last":162,"ar":"يا بَنِي إِسْرائِيلَ اذْكُرُوا نِعْمَتِيَ الَّتِي أَنْعَمْتُ عَلَيْكُمْ وَأَنِّي فَضَّلْتُكُمْ عَلَى الْعالَمِينَ (122) وَاتَّقُوا يَوْماً لا تَجْزِي نَفْسٌ عَنْ نَفْسٍ شَيْئاً وَلا يُقْبَلُ مِنْها عَدْلٌ وَلا تَنْفَعُها شَفاعَةٌ وَلا هُمْ يُنْصَرُونَ (123)\nقلت: جملة (لا تجزي) : نعت ليوم، وحذف العائد، أي: لا تجزي فيه نفس، قال المرادي: (إذا نعت بالجملة اسم زمان جاز حذف عائده) ثم استدل بالآية. وهل حذف برمته أو بالتدريج؟ قولان.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: يا بَنِي إِسْرائِيلَ اذْكُرُوا نِعْمَتِيَ الَّتِي أَنْعَمْتُ عَلَيْكُمْ بأن جعلت الأنبياء تسوسكم، والملوك منكم يدبرون أموركم، وفَضَّلْتُكُمْ على عالم زمانكم، فاشكروا هذه النعم بالإيمان بالرسول الذي أرسلته إليكم، وخافوا أهوال يوم القيامة، الذي لا تغنى فيه نَفْسٌ عَنْ نَفْسٍ شَيْئاً وَلا يُقْبَلُ مِنْها فداء إن أرادت الفداء، وَلا تَنْفَعُها شَفاعَةٌ\nشافع، ولا يدفع عنها أهوال ذلك اليوم ولي ولا ناصر، إلا من اتخذ يداً عند الملك القادر، وبالله التوفيق. وتقدمت إشارة هذه الآية في الآية الأولى.\nوَلَمَّا أراد الحقُّ تعالى أن ينسخ القبلة ويردها إلى بيت الله الحرام بعد أن كانت إلى بيت المقدس، ذكر خصوصيةَ مَنْ بناه، وكيفية بنائه، وفي ضمن ذلك ذكر شرفه ليكون ذلك داعياً إلى الامتثال، فقال:","quran_ar":"يا بَنِي إِسْرائِيلَ اذْكُرُوا نِعْمَتِيَ الَّتِي أَنْعَمْتُ عَلَيْكُمْ وَأَنِّي فَضَّلْتُكُمْ عَلَى الْعالَمِينَ (122) وَاتَّقُوا يَوْماً لا تَجْزِي نَفْسٌ عَنْ نَفْسٍ شَيْئاً وَلا يُقْبَلُ مِنْها عَدْلٌ وَلا تَنْفَعُها شَفاعَةٌ وَلا هُمْ يُنْصَرُونَ (123)","quran_en":"122. Children of Israel, remember My blessing which I bestowed upon you, and that I favoured you above the worlds. 123. Be mindful of a day when no soul can avail another at all, no ransom will be accepted from it, no intercession will benefit it, and they will not be helped.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Omitting the resumptive pronoun","html":"<p>I say: “no soul can avail…” qualifies “a day.” Its resumptive expression is omitted: “a day in which no soul can avail…”. Al-Murādī says that when a temporal noun is qualified by a sentence, its resumptive may be omitted, citing this verse. Was the whole expression omitted at once or in stages? There are two views.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “no soul can avail…” qualifies “a day.” Its resumptive expression is omitted: “a day in which no soul can avail…”. Al-Murādī says that when a temporal noun is qualified by a sentence, its resumptive may be omitted, citing this verse. Was the whole expression omitted at once or in stages? There are two views."},{"type":"exposition","title":"The favour of prophets and kings","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: Children of Israel, remember My blessing: I appointed prophets to guide you and kings from among you to manage your affairs, and favoured you above the people of your time. Thank Me by believing the messenger I sent to you. Fear the terrors of the Day of Resurrection, when no soul can avail another, no ransom is accepted should it offer one, and no intercessor benefits it. No protector or helper repels that day’s terrors, except for one who has established a claim of favour with the Powerful King. Through God is success. The spiritual allusion of this verse has already appeared at its first occurrence [2:47–48].<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: Children of Israel, remember My blessing: I appointed prophets to guide you and kings from among you to manage your affairs, and favoured you above the people of your time. Thank Me by believing the messenger I sent to you. Fear the terrors of the Day of Resurrection, when no soul can avail another, no ransom is accepted should it offer one, and no intercessor benefits it. No protector or helper repels that day’s terrors, except for one who has established a claim of favour with the Powerful King. Through God is success. The spiritual allusion of this verse has already appeared at its first occurrence [2:47–48]."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>When the Real wished to change the prayer-direction from Jerusalem back to God’s Sacred House, He mentioned the special distinction of its builder and the manner of its building. Within this He set forth its honour, encouraging compliance, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"When the Real wished to change the prayer-direction from Jerusalem back to God’s Sacred House, He mentioned the special distinction of its builder and the manner of its building. Within this He set forth its honour, encouraging compliance, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":1223,"arabic_characters":1132}];
